######
#
# Libatsdoc: the library of the programs that atsdoc generates
#
######

atsdoc (../utils/atsdoc) turns a .atxt file into an ATS2 program and a text; the
program, linked with libatsdoc, puts the "atexts" that the placeholders stand for
in theAtextMap and prints the text with the placeholders replaced (fprint_filsub).
The interface is SATS/libatsdoc_atext.sats (atext_strcst, atext_strsub,
atext_apptxt2/3, atext_appstr2/3, atext_concatxt, atext_concatxtsep, atext_newline,
atext_strptr, atext_filepath, theAtextMap_insert_str, theAtextMap_search,
fprint_atext, fprint_strsub, fprint_filsub, file2strptr, tostring_fprint, ...) and
SATS/libatsdoc_symbol.sats.

It is written in ATS2 without GC: an atext is a handle into the global arena
(libatsdoc_atext.spec, utils/arenagen) that is never freed, a list of atexts is a
glist (atext_concatxt_vt takes a list_vt), and the strings that are given to the
constructors are copied into the arena. The differences from the ATS1 version are
listed at the top of SATS/libatsdoc_atext.sats and in src/PORTING.md ("atsdoc and
libatsdoc"). libatsdoc.a needs nothing but libats/arena and libc (the table of the
symbols is in C, CATS/libatsdoc_symbol.cats): it can be linked with libatsopt.a and
libatsynmark.a (-latsynmark -latsdoc -latsopt -lgmp).

######

Building (ATS1 is not needed):

  make                  # BUILD/libatsdoc.a
  make install          # copies it to $(PATSHOME)/ccomp/atslib/lib (`make -C ../utils/atsdoc` does it)
  make check            # typechecks the files
  make gen              # regenerates the arena encoding of the atext tree (python3)

Using it (the pipeline of the .atxt files):

  atsdoc --outcode X_atxt.dats -i X.atxt > X_atxt.txt
  patscc -DATS_MEMALLOC_LIBC -o X_atxt.exe X_atxt.dats -latsdoc
  ./X_atxt.exe > X

[dynload "libatsdoc/dynloadall.dats"] (or a call of libatsdoc_dynloadall(), whose
name does not depend on any path) initializes the library. The name of the function
that the dynload statement calls contains the path of dynloadall.dats, so the
programs have to be compiled with the PATSHOME that libatsdoc.a was built with.

The tests are in ../utils/atsdoc/TEST (make -C ../utils/atsdoc test).

######
